<article xml:id="session3wks" label="3401">

  <title>MATH 142 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <worksheet>

    <title>Session #3 (1.1 - 1.4, 2.1 - 2.2)</title>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Getting Warmed Up and In the Right Mindset</title>
        <p>
        Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you. 
        For additional practice problems and explanations, refer to the Math 142 textbook which is free and can be downloaded as a PDF 
        at <url href="https://oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/03980291-5e85-4d2e-a742-961ac49666aa"> Texas A&amp;M The OAKTrust Digital Repository </url>.
        </p>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            State the formula for finding the average rate of change of a function <m>f</m> on the interval <m>[a,b]</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is also known as the slope of the secant line.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the average rate of <m>f(x)=x^2+5x</m> on the interval <m>[2,3]</m>. Draw the line that represents this average rate of change on the graph.
          </p>
          <image source="MATH142_HOGU_Session3_Fig2.png" width="45%"/>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            State the formula for the difference quotient of a function <m>f</m> at <m>x=a</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compute the difference quotient of <m>f(x)=\sqrt{x}</m> at <m>x=4</m> with <m>h=0.1</m>. (Round your answer to four decimal places, if needed).
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>

      <title>Let us get our hands on!</title>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="1">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            The cost of a company to produce <m>x</m> products is given by <m>C(x)=0.04x^2+130</m> dollars.
          </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Calculate the average rate of change of <m>f</m> over each of the following intervals.
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li><p><m>[95,100]</m></p></li>
              <li><p><m>[97,100]</m></p></li>
              <li><p><m>[99,100]</m></p></li>
              <li><p><m>[100,101]</m></p></li>
            </ol>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Use part(a) to approximate the instantaneous rate of change when <m>100</m> products are produced. Interpret your results.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="2">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Suppose we want to find the instantaneous rate of change of <m>f(x)=x^2+5x</m> at <m>x=3</m>, or <m>f'(3)</m>:
          </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Circle the correct formula for <m>f'(3)</m>:
            </p>
            <p>
              <m>
                f'(3)=\lim_{h \to 3}\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}
              </m>
              OR
              <m>
                f'(3)=\lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}
              </m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Follow the steps below to compute <m>f'(3)</m>.
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Calculate <m>f(3+h)</m> and combine like terms.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Calculate <m>f(3+h)-f(3)</m> and combine like terms.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Calculate the difference quotient
                  <m>\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}</m>
                  and simplify to get rid of <m>h</m> on the bottom.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Take the limit <m>h \to 0</m> of your simplified expression in iii. Label the resulting value as <m>f'(3)</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="3">
        <statement>
          <p>
            In #6 we found that <m>f'(3)=11</m>. Use this to find the equation of the line tangent to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="4">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Suppose we want to find the instantaneous rate of change of <m>f(x)=3x^2-x</m> at any <m>x</m>, not just at a specified <m>x</m> value like in Problem 5.
          </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write the correct formula for <m>f'(x)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Follow the same steps as in Problem 5, part(b) to compute <m>f'(x)</m>. Note: Because we are not looking at a specific <m>x</m> value, your final answer may have an <m>x</m> in it!
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>f'(2)</m> using your answer from part(b).
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="5">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find <m>f'(4)</m>, given <m>f(x)=2\sqrt{x}</m>.
            Hint: Multiply by the conjugate in step iii and simplify.
            If you don't do this, then taking the limit <m>h \to 0</m> in step iv gives you the indeterminate form <m>\frac{0}{0}</m> which we can't work with.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="6">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Use the graph of <m>p(x)</m> below to answer questions #6-8.
          </p>
          <image source="MATH142_HOGU_Session3_Fig6.png" width="50%"/>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the following limits.
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li><p><m>\lim_{x\rightarrow -4} p(x)</m></p></li>
              <li><p><m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 2} p(x)</m></p></li>
              <li><p><m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 10^-} p(x)</m></p></li>
              <li><p><m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 8^+} p(x)</m></p></li>
            </ol>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find all <m>x</m>-values where <m>p(x)</m> is discontinuous. For each <m>x</m>-value, state the condition (I, II, or III) within the definition of continuity that fails first.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find all <m>x</m>-values where the derivative of <m>p(x)</m> does not exist and give a reason for why the derivative does not exist at that <m>x</m>-value.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="7">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The graph of <m>f(x)</m> is given below.
          </p>
          <image source="Session3_Image1.png" width="30%"/>
          <p>
            Which of the following is the graph of <m>f'(x)</m>?
          </p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <image source="Session3_Image4.png" width="30%"/>
            </li>
            <li>
              <image source="Session3_Image5.png" width="30%"/>
            </li>
            <li>
              <image source="Session3_Image2.png" width="30%"/>
            </li>
            <li>
              <image source="Session3_Image3.png" width="30%"/>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>

      <title>Now...Grades Go Up!</title>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine whether
            <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow -1} \frac{1}{x^2-1}</m>
            exists. If it does exist, find the limit. If it does not exist, describe the behavior of the function at <m>x=-1</m>.
          </p>

          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p>The limit exists, and it equals <m>0</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The limit exists, and it equals <m>-\frac{1}{2}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The limit exists, and it equals <m>1</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The limit does not exist, but
                <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow -1^-} f(x) \rightarrow \infty</m>
                and
                <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow -1^+} f(x) \rightarrow -\infty</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The limit does not exist, but
                <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow -1^-} f(x) \rightarrow -\infty</m>
                and
                <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow -1^+} f(x) \rightarrow \infty</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find
            <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 5} \frac{x^2-x-20}{x^2-25}</m>.
          </p>

          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p><m>0</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>1</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\text{DNE}</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\frac{4}{5}</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\frac{9}{10}</m></p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="3">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the horizontal asymptotes for
            <m>f(x)=\frac{6e^{-x}+8e^{-3x}+6}{7e^{-2x}-2+2e^{-3x}}</m>
          </p>

          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p><m>f(x)</m> does not have any horizontal asymptotes.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>f(x)</m> has two horizontal asymptotes at <m>y=-3</m> and <m>y=4</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>f(x)</m> has only one horizontal asymptote at <m>y=-3</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>f(x)</m> has only one horizontal asymptote at <m>y=0</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>f(x)</m> has only one horizontal asymptote at <m>y=4</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="4">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the intervals where <m>f(x)</m> is continuous.
          </p>

          <md>
            f(x)=
            \left\{
            \begin{array}{lc}
            \frac{x^2-9}{x-3} &amp; x &lt; 4 \\
            \sqrt{10x+9} &amp; x &gt; 4
            \end{array}
            \right.
          </md>

          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p><m>(-\infty, 3) \cup (3,\infty)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>(-\infty, 3) \cup (3,4) \cup (4,\infty)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>(-\infty,4) \cup (4,\infty)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>(-\infty,\infty)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>(-\infty,-\frac{9}{10}) \cup (-\frac{9}{10},3) \cup (3,4) \cup (4,\infty)</m></p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="5">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose for some function <m>g(x)</m>, you know
            <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 8^-} g(x) \rightarrow -\infty</m>.
            What can you conclude happens at <m>x=8</m>?
          </p>

          <ol marker="(A)">
            <li><p><m>g(x)</m> has a vertical asymptote at <m>x=8</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>g(x)</m> has a horizontal asymptote at <m>x=8</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>g(x)</m> has a derivative at <m>x=8</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>g(x)</m> has a hole at <m>x=8</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>g(8)\rightarrow\infty</m></p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="6">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given
            <m>p(x)=\frac{7x(x+8)(x+1)^2}{5x^2(x+1)(x+8)^2}</m>,
            find all vertical asymptotes for <m>p(x)</m>.
          </p>

          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p><m>x=0</m>, <m>x=-1</m>, and <m>x=-8</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x=-1</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x=-1</m> and <m>x=-8</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>y=\frac{7}{5}</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x=0</m>, <m>x=-8</m></p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="7">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given
            <m>p(x)=\frac{7x(x+8)(x+1)^2}{5x^2(x+1)(x+8)^2}</m>,
            find the <m>x</m>-values where all holes occur for <m>p(x)</m>.
          </p>

          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p><m>x=-1</m> and <m>x=-8</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x=0</m>, <m>x=-1</m>, and <m>x=-8</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x=-1</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x=0</m> and <m>x=-8</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>p(x)</m> does not have any holes.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in" number="8">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine whether each statement is true or false.
          </p>

          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                True/False: If <m>g(x)</m> is a rational function with
                <m>\lim_{x \to \infty} g(x) = -\frac{1}{2}</m>,
                then it must be that
                <m>\lim_{x \to -\infty} g(x) = -\frac{1}{2}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                True/False: If a function <m>f(x)</m> is not differentiable at <m>x=3</m>, then it must not be continuous at <m>x=3</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                True/False: If a function <m>f(x)</m> is continuous at <m>x=-1</m> then it must also be differentiable at <m>x=-1</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                True/False: If <m>h(x)</m> is a rational function with
                <m>\lim_{x \to \infty} h(x) \to -\infty</m>,
                then it must also be that
                <m>\lim_{x \to -\infty} h(x) \to -\infty</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

    </paragraphs>

  </worksheet>

</article>

</pretext>